-- title:
--   Theorem 3.4 — every accumulation point of the partial sparse-simplex method is an L₂(f)-stationary point
-- statement:
--   Let $f:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R$ be continuously differentiable and bounded below (Assumption 1), with Lipschitz gradient (Assumption 2), and let $0<s<n$ be an integer. Let $L_2>0$ satisfy the local Lipschitz condition (2.15): $\|\nabla_{i,j}f(x)-\nabla_{i,j}f(x+d)\|\le L_2\|d\|$ for all $i\ne j$, all $x$ and all $d$ supported in $\{i,j\}$. Let $(x^k)$ be a sequence generated by the partial sparse-simplex method.
--
--   Then every accumulation point $x^*$ of $(x^k)$ is an $L_2$-stationary point of (P):
--   $$x^*\in C_s\qquad\text{and}\qquad x^*\in P_{C_s}\!\Big(x^*-\frac1{L_2}\nabla f(x^*)\Big).$$
--
--   The partial sparse-simplex method examines only two candidate moves per saturated iteration, far fewer than the greedy method's $O(sn)$, yet its limit points satisfy $L_2(f)$-stationarity, which is stronger than the $L$-stationarity with $L>L(f)$ guaranteed for iterative hard thresholding, because $L_2(f)\le L(f)$ can be much smaller.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Accumulation point" is `MapClusterPt xstar atTop x`: $x^*$ is a cluster point of the sequence. $L_2$ is any positive constant satisfying (2.15), in particular the paper's $L_2(f)$ when it is positive; positivity is needed because the method's analysis divides by $L_2$. Assumption 2 is kept as on the page; the argument uses it only through (2.15). The method is the box of p. 25 with its two misprints corrected (see the definition of the method); a STOP is modelled by the sequence staying at the stopping point.
-- source:
--   Beck, Eldar, Sparsity Constrained Nonlinear Optimization: Optimality Conditions and Algorithms, SIAM J. Optim. (2013), doi:10.1137/120869778 (source: arXiv:1203.4580v1), p. 26, Theorem 3.4 (proof §4.2.3, pp. 30–33)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SparseNLO_Partial_Method

open Filter Topology

namespace SparseNLO.Partial

/-- Theorem 3.4 (p. 26): suppose Assumption 2 holds and let `L2 > 0` satisfy the local
Lipschitz condition (2.15). Then every accumulation point `x*` of a sequence generated by the
partial sparse-simplex method is an `L2`-stationary point (Definition 2.3 with `L = L₂(f)`). -/
theorem theorem_3_4 {n s : ℕ} (hs : 0 < s) (hsn : s < n)
    (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (hf : ContDiff ℝ 1 f) (hbdd : ∃ γ : ℝ, ∀ x, γ ≤ f x)
    (Lf : NNReal) (hLf : LipschitzWith Lf (gradient f))
    (L2 : ℝ) (hL2pos : 0 < L2) (hL2 : LocalLipschitz f L2)
    (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hrun : IsPartialRun f s x)
    (xstar : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hacc : MapClusterPt xstar atTop x) :
    IsLStationary f s L2 xstar := by sorry

end SparseNLO.Partial
